 ],
 "literal": "Nalli of the lofty mountain with roaring high waterfalls, your / unwearied strong effort and desired wealth praising, / every day fine ornaments with elephants bringing, / in the wide mansion where the assembly shines, as you bestow gifts entirely, / desiring the praise of kings without greatness, / speaking of things not done and uttering, / has become not known to our small perfect tongue indeed.",
 "translation": "O Naḷḷi, lord of the lofty mountain with roaring high waterfalls! Praising your unwearied, strong efforts and your desired wealth, you bring fine ornaments along with elephants every single day to your wide, shining mansion and bestow them completely as gifts. Because of this, our small, honest tongue has never known what it is to praise worthless kings, inventing deeds they never performed.",
 "modern": "ஒலிக்கும் உயரமான அருவிகளைக் கொண்ட உயர்ந்த மலையையுடைய நள்ளியே! உன்னுடைய சோர்வில்லாத வலிமையான முயற்சியையும் விரும்பத்தக்க செல்வத்தையும் புகழ்ந்து பாடி, நாள்தோறும் நல்ல அணிகலன்களையும் யானைகளையும் கொண்டு வந்து, சுற்றம் விளங்கும் உனது அகன்ற மாளிகையில் நீ பரிசில்களை முழுமையாக வழங்குவதால், பெருமையில்லாத பிற மன்னர்களைப் புகழ்ந்து பெற விரும்பி, அவர்கள் செய்யாத காரியங்களைச் செய்ததாகப் பொய் கூறிப் பேசுவதை எங்களது சிறிய செம்மையான நா அறியாததாகிவிட்டது.",
 "explanation": "This poem is in the Pāṭāṇ tiṇai, where the poet Vaṉparaṇar praises the patron Kaṇṭīrakakōp Peru-naḷḷi. The poet describes Naḷḷi's mountain as a place of roaring waterfalls, symbolizing his abundant resources. Naḷḷi is so generous that he brings fine ornaments and elephants daily to distribute to those who visit his mansion. Because of Naḷḷi's boundless and complete generosity, the poet's tongue has never had to stoop to praising minor, worthless kings by fabricating false achievements. The key words 'செந்நா' (honest/perfect tongue) and 'எய்யாதாகின்று' (has not known) emphasize the dignity that Naḷḷi's patronage preserves for the poets.",
